=== Credits
Units: 4 (ELEC 400O, MECH 410Q, MECH 540G). Letter grade.

=== Course Description

This course is an introduction to optimization for engineers, with an emphasis on algorithms: how they are derived, why they converge, and how they behave on real problems. Topics include:

- Mathematical background: linear algebra, conditioning, and multivariable calculus
- Formulating and reformulating engineering problems as optimization problems
- Optimality conditions (unconstrained and constrained), duality, and sensitivity
- Algorithms: gradient descent, line search, Newton and damped Newton, projected gradient, interior-point methods, and sequential quadratic programming

=== Learning Objectives

Students completing this course should be able to:

- Recognise the class an optimization problem belongs to, and turn an engineering problem into mathematical optimization problem
- State the optimality conditions appropriate to a problem, and use them to certify or reject a candidate solution
- Derive the main algorithms of the course and explain the assumptions each one needs
- Implement basic optimization algorithms, and diagnose their failure modes
- Explain how conditioning and numerical errors affect the behaviour of an optimization algorithm
- Use an off-the-shelf solver competently and interpret what it reports


=== Course Schedule (_tentative_)

- *Week 1*: Introduction
- *Week 2-3*: Mathematical foundations 
- *Week 4*: Optimization problems: overview and modelling
- *Week 5-7*: Unconstrained optimization: theory and algorithms
- *Week 8-12*: Constrained optimization: theory and algorithms
- *Week 12-13*: Bonus topics / Final project presentations

*No classes*: 30 September (National Day for Truth and Reconciliation), and 10-11 November (midterm break and Remembrance Day). 15-18 September (instructor away), and _possibly_ 9-16 October (instructor _possibly_ away).

