=== Material & References

All lecture materials — slides, annotated slides, exercises, and notebooks — are available in [https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fo/zgzbwp2s87ujgxdn27r80/ALPxBv9N3kF8u05kH__CbLM?rlkey=p0aplx43mai7638jvuw1fz5hg&st=0wjdc9mw&dl=0 this online folder]. New materials are added before each lecture or shortly afterwards.

- J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright, /Numerical Optimization/. 2nd ed., Springer, 2006.
- S. Boyd and L. Vandenberghe, /Convex Optimization/. Cambridge University Press, 2004. (optional)
- D. P. Bertsekas, /Nonlinear Programming/. 3rd ed., Athena Scientific, 2016. (optional)

See also the [https://www.albertopadoan.com/resources.html Resources page].

=== Prerequisites

# TODO: replace the three bullets with the official course prerequisite?

- Linear algebra and multivariable calculus at the level of a second-year engineering course.
- Familiarity with programming (needed for both tutorials and final project).
- No prior exposure to optimization is assumed.

=== Assessment

*ELEC 400-O and MECH 410-Q*
- 35% -- Midterm 1 
- 35% -- Midterm 2 
- 30% -- Final project 

*MECH 540-G*
- 30% -- Midterm 1 
- 30% -- Midterm 2 
- 30% -- Final project 
- 10% -- Research presentation (last 2 weeks of lectures)

*Note*: Use of generative AI is permitted as a tool. However, all submitted work must reflect the student’s own understanding. Work primarily generated by AI will receive a grade of zero.

=== Final Project
A short research-style report and a runnable Python notebook, taking one method from the course further than the lectures do. Main idea: identify a problem for which standard formulations or off-the-shelf algorithms fall short, develop a formulation or algorithm based on material from the course, and demonstrate its effectiveness through numerical experiments, theoretical analysis, or both. Groups of two are preferred.
 
Benchmark problems may be suggested by the instructors, but original topics are especially welcome.

*Timeline*
- *Topic proposal* -- 20 October, one paragraph
- *Project talks* -- From 18 November, during the tutorial hour
- *Report and notebook* -- due 4 December, 16:00

Full details, including the report and presentation rubrics and the academic concession policy, are in the [https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fo/7gq3ffzgsju3okvb5zzwu/AMq_q86wT8NTOvRB5HdMGhU?rlkey=0a9emrp7cc0l2qz379pssyy1b&st=aks196wt&dl=0 Grading Policy].

=== Late policy

Deadlines are firm — late work or missed assessments will not be graded, consistent with the [https://vancouver.calendar.ubc.ca/campus-wide-policies-and-regulations/grading-practices Academic Calendar on Grading Practices]. If illness or compassionate grounds prevent you from sitting a midterm or meeting the project deadline, contact the instructor in writing as early as possible; requests are handled under the UBC policy on [https://vancouver.calendar.ubc.ca/campus-wide-policies-and-regulations/academic-concession academic concession].


